WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
Speaker 1: All right. I want to&nbsp;
talk next about the things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.200
different people are doing in response&nbsp;
to what's going on with the coronavirus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.960
pandemic. There are people who in their&nbsp;
place of business, if they control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.520
if they have a place of business and they&nbsp;
have control over what goes on, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.560
are saying my employees need to be vaccinated&nbsp;
or my customers need to be vaccinated or you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.240
need a negative test if you're not vaccinated&nbsp;
or whatever. One Alabama doctor is now saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:32.640
he is not going to treat unvaccinated people. He&nbsp;
will not see unvaccinated patients in his office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:40.160
Dr. Jason Valentine is a physician at Mobile's&nbsp;
Diagnostic and Medical Clinic. Infirmary Health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:47.360
posted a photo on his Facebook page showing him&nbsp;
next to a sign that says Effective October 1st.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:53.360
Twenty twenty one, Dr. Valentine will no longer&nbsp;
see patients that are not vaccinated against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:01:00.000
covid-19. Since posting the sign, Valentine&nbsp;
wrote, three unvaccinated patients asked where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.640
they could get a vaccine. No conspiracy theories,&nbsp;
no excuses. Just where do they go? Valentine had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.520
a blunt answer from those who asked him about his&nbsp;
decision. If they asked why, I told them covid is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.720
a miserable way to die and I can't watch them die&nbsp;
like that, Valentine said. He's currently in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.280
process of mailing a letter to patients about&nbsp;
his decision. He posted a copy of the letter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:29.200
online. We do not yet have any great treatments&nbsp;
for severe disease, he wrote. But we do have great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.400
prevention with vaccines. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
many have declined to take the vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:40.560
and some end up severely ill or dead. I cannot&nbsp;
and will not force anyone to take the vaccine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.360
but I also cannot continue to watch my patients&nbsp;
suffer and die from an eminently preventable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.000
disease. Therefore, as of October 1st, I will no&nbsp;
longer see patients that have not been vaccinated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.280
against covid-19. If you wish to keep me as your&nbsp;
physician, documentation of your vaccination will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:01.200
suffice. If you wish to choose another physician,&nbsp;
we will be happy to transfer your records. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.320
a bunch of people wrote to me about the story and&nbsp;
said, Isn't this a violation of the Hippocratic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.760
Oath? Don't doctors have a duty to treat those&nbsp;
who are in need of treatment? And the answer is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.920
yes. But this is not an emergency physician.&nbsp;
This is someone who this is primary care. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:24.800
is someone who's saying in my office, my roster&nbsp;
of patients will not include unvaccinated people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:32.160
And this is not emergency medicine. This is not&nbsp;
a doctor having someone showing up and needing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:38.320
be put on a ventilator in an emergency room.&nbsp;
And he says, now I'm going to look away. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.160
not going to provide you any treatment. This is&nbsp;
not emergency medicine. This is. Primary care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.400
so it's focusing on the Hippocratic&nbsp;
Oath aspect of this is not an honest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:54.800
assessment of the situation. What we do need to&nbsp;
be thinking about here is that there is a burden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.800
that is larger than it needs to be being placed&nbsp;
on the medical system, being placed on providers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.160
doctors, nurses and others. And it is a&nbsp;
perfectly reasonable thing to say. Wait a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:12.880
if much of this the vaccine's not perfect, OK,&nbsp;
you could still get a case with the vaccine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.640
You could end up in the hospital. You could&nbsp;
end up dying even when vaccinated. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:25.200
dramatically reduces your odds. So if we have&nbsp;
that as a possibility, what else can we be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:31.600
doing? What else can doctors be doing to say the&nbsp;
burden needs to be reduced? Let's find a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.520
encourage people to go and get vaccinated. Now,&nbsp;
I took a call on the show a couple of weeks ago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:43.600
where the viewer actually said shouldn't when we&nbsp;
triage in an emergency room, take unvaccinated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:50.800
people and triage them lower priority than&nbsp;
vaccinated people so that we don't say to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:56.640
person who got into a car accident, your treatment&nbsp;
will be delayed because of unvaccinated people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.080
And my answer was no, I don't see any way that you&nbsp;
do that legally. I don't see any way that you do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:07.280
that morally or ethically from the standpoint&nbsp;
of when you're in an emergency room situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:14.080
you're triaging based on severity and other&nbsp;
factors. And I don't think that that would make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:20.000
sense. But increasingly, if primary care doctors&nbsp;
want to say, hey, listen, at my office, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.880
want to be under my care, under primary care, if&nbsp;
you want to come in because you might have a sinus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.400
infection or you have, you know, headache or you&nbsp;
have whatever reason people go into primary care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:37.360
you need to be vaccinated because I need to&nbsp;
reduce the burden, then that is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.840
that they can do. And many doctors could could do&nbsp;
this. And ultimately, people are going to have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:47.440
make a decision. Do I want to have a primary care&nbsp;
doctor and get the vaccine or do I want to remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.440
unvaccinated and maybe be unable to find one? Now,&nbsp;
of course, most primary care doctors are not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.640
to do this. So it's really academic to say what&nbsp;
if every primary care doctor did this? But good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:01.520
for this doctor. This doctor is doing something&nbsp;
to try to encourage people to get vaccinated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.680
At least three of his patients have said, I'm&nbsp;
going to go and do it because I want to remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:12.160
under your care. Send me your thoughts about this,&nbsp;
as well as other ways that the medical community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.560
could encourage people to go and get vaccinated.&nbsp;
